data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining the Research Scope**\n\nOkay, I've got a handle on the task. My focus is now narrowed to answering six French research questions in order: 1, 2, 3, 9, 15, and 18. This involves ensuring the answers are well-formatted, and will be presented with a title. I'm ready to begin the research.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 2065,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 351,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "j-gCa6CNNq_-5NoPrKTj2Qs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining the Response Format**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the response formatting, specifically the titles and word count. The title for each answer *must* be exactly \"Réponse k\", with k being the answer number, placed on its own line. I’m thinking plain text for the titles is safest. Word count is now set as my guide, with each response targeting 450-550 words to avoid exceeding the 600-word limit.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 2150,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 436,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "j-gCa6CNNq_-5NoPrKTj2Qs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Verifying Word Count Limits**\n\nI'm now deep-diving on word count restrictions in the French responses. To be absolutely certain, I'm aiming for between 480 and 520 apostrophe-split tokens per answer to avoid issues with different word counters. I am confident that is a good range that will enable each sub-question to be answered thoroughly without any issues.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 2464,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 750,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "j-gCa6CNNq_-5NoPrKTj2Qs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Novelty Criteria**\n\nI'm now grappling with the core questions. I'm focusing on defining what constitutes a \"new category\" for language models. I'm considering measurable criteria, such as the failure of defensive transfer and invariance of abstraction. I'm thinking about how these criteria would manifest in experiments. I'm exploring defenses against attacks. I'm still working on an experimental framework using smaller, open models.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 2698,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 984,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "j-gCa6CNNq_-5NoPrKTj2Qs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Novelty Categories**\n\nI'm now diving into the specifics. A novel category requires an attack that remains effective even after adapting the model to known categories (orthogonal risk gradient) AND needs observation of a channel or state variable absent from the input space (incomplete observation interface) to be corrected. I'm looking at historical examples, such as timing/power attacks on cryptosystems, and speculative execution attacks like Spectre and Meltdown, which exploit these very qualities.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 2970,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1256,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "j-gCa6CNNq_-5NoPrKTj2Qs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Recalling Related Literature**\n\nI'm now cataloging relevant literature to support my claims about novelty. I'm recalling key papers, including Paul Kocher's work on timing attacks and Spectre, Anderson and Needham on programming security, and Landwehr et al.'s taxonomy of flaws, all from memory due to my circumstances. I'm focusing on how these examples demonstrate violations of architectural contracts, a key element in my definition of novelty. I'm noting the specific years and titles to ensure accuracy, despite relying on memory.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 3278,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1564,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "j-gCa6CNNq_-5NoPrKTj2Qs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating the Feedback Loop**\n\nI'm now clarifying the nature of feedback loops in language model groups, focusing on identifying true novel categories. I'm hypothesizing that fixed LLM groups primarily generate variants or recombined training patterns unless connected to a real-world executor that exposes an unmodeled channel through feedback. I'm planning controls: one involves searching the training corpus for structural isomorphisms in the training data, and the second involves transfer testing. I'm focusing on a first experiment using a smaller open model to test my hypothesis.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 3523,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1809,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "j-gCa6CNNq_-5NoPrKTj2Qs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Constructing a Synthetic Sandbox**\n\nI'm now outlining a synthetic sandbox for multi-stage analysis. My plan is to train detectors (linear probes on activations, text judge, partial audit test) on known sabotage families. I'll use RL/evolution on a smaller model to find a policy that degrades the final score. I'll then measure the cross-detection rate, and retrain the existing probes/judges on traces from the candidate without changing their observation space. The detection of a new category will be determined if the ASR remains high until the addition of a new observable class.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 3798,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2084,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "j-gCa6CNNq_-5NoPrKTj2Qs"}

